)
modelsummary(
list("Model 1: Unadjusted" = m1, "Model 2: Adjusted" = m2),
coef_map   = coef_map,
gof_map    = gof_map,
statistic  = "conf.int",
conf_level = 0.95,
stars      = c("*"=.05,"**"=.01,"***"=.001),
output     = "latex",
title      = "Linear regression models predicting GAD-7 anxiety score. 95\\% CIs in brackets.",
notes      = "Reference categories: LGBTQ+ identity = Cis-Hetero; First-Gen = Not First-Gen.",
booktabs   = TRUE,
escape     = FALSE
)
Several limitations warrant consideration. First, thecross-sectional designprevents causal inference orthe establishment of temporal ordering. Higher internet use may contribute to anxiety, but students withelevated anxiety may also increase online activity as avoidance behavior — a bidirectional relationship thatcannot be disentangled here. Second,all variables were self-reported, introducing potential recall andsocial-desirability biases. Third,internet_1capturestotal screen timewithout distinguishing betweenacademic, social, passive, or active engagement — distinctions with meaningfully different psychologicalimplications. Fourth,listwise deletionfor missing covariates may introduce bias if data are not missingcompletely at random. Fifth,non-response weightswere not applied, which may limit strict generaliz-ability. Finally, despite the extensive covariate set,residual confoundingfrom unmeasured variables (e.g.,loneliness, sleep quality, stressful life events) cannot be ruled out.
